A||||7||webbee||Auto shift kits?||||1||
Z||000000||webbee||04-22-2001||12:57 AM||||I was talking to a mechanic friend and he advised against shift kits. He says that yes they prolong the clutch pack life but they cause early seal failure, due to increased pressure during shifting. Anybody have a shift kit installed for +40k miles or had any trouble with a tranny that has had a shift kit?  I would like to add one when I rebuild but don't want problems later.||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||04-22-2001||03:36 PM||||It's been my experience that the mojority of people putting kits in beat the crap out of the cars. I'd imajine that's got allot more to do with it than the kit.<P><BR>before spending on a kit try putting a new filter and DexronIII in it. That and making sure the TV cable are set right go along way...||||reg||1
Z||000002||grinthock||04-22-2001||10:29 PM||||I was advised by the Fiero Shop here in Toronto and 2 other tranny shops that Shift kits cause performance increases but add increased wear, they said the performance increase was not as great as you might seem.<P>I put a higher stall Torque Converter in my auto made a HUGE difference.||||reg||3
Z||000003||ray b||04-22-2001||11:10 PM||||grinthock what stall speed and make torkconv.<BR>cost? how much does it affect milage?||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||04-22-2001||11:18 PM||||As long as the TC locks properly, stall speed shouldn't make a diferance in milage. It will only effect "off the line" performance. Once it's cruising in 3 then the thing will lock and is eliminated from the equation.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Oreif||04-23-2001||09:46 AM||||There are 2 kits of "shift kits" available for the auto's in the Fiero's. There is the performance one and the Heavy Duty one. I have the Heavy Duty one. It is the same parts that GM used for the 6000/Celebrity cars.<BR>Trans-Go makes it. Basically it makes the trans shift better (not so much "harder") and it prevents the torque converter from "bleeding" down in a corner. <BR>IRM use to sell the original GM kit but now Trans-Go makes it. (not sure if anyone else makes the kit) If anything get the anti-bleed down for the torque converter. If you do autocrossing or scream around corners, this will add life to the trans. (Hard cornering forces fluid out of torque converter and deceases pressure. This can cause trans to slip during the cornering.)||||reg||1
Z||000006||webbee||04-23-2001||03:24 PM||||Thank's guys! <P>Oreif, can you steer me to a supplier for the heavy duty kit?<P>Grinthock, what was the new stall speed?<P>I am about to do a rebuild and would like to beef it up. I am a hard cornerer but not an autocrosser. I don't want to end up doing the job again due to adding racing parts that increase wear.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Oreif||04-23-2001||07:04 PM||||You can get the Trans-Go kit from anyone who sells them. I think Pep Boys and Trak can order them. You want the TH125 Heavy Duty kit. I'll look tonight and see if I can locate the kit number.<P>Here's the info:<BR>P/N: SK 125<BR>THM 125 <BR>1980 up <BR>Corrects/prevents/Reduces: <BR>3-2 clunk; lockup shudder; reverse slip; converter drain back delay; <BR>late shifts; soft 1-2; band failure. <P><BR>P/N: 125-S <BR>THM 125 <BR>l980 up <BR>2nd Servo Cushion Spring (OEM replacement) <P>Here's the link of what/how the kit works:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.txchange.com/transgo.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.txchange.com/transgo.htm</A><P>You can order from them at: <A HREF="http://www.txchange.com/order.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.txchange.com/order.htm</A>  <P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 04-23-2001).]||||reg||1
